---
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
import DateRange from "../../components/DateRange";
import SkillList from "../../components/SkillList";
import CTABanner from "../../components/Home/CTABanner";
import projects from "../../data/projects.json";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import image_thunderbird_pose from "../../images/thunderbird_pose.jpg";
import image_thunderbird_sea from "../../images/thunderbird_sea.jpg";
import image_ubc_sailbot_team from "../../images/ubc_sailbot_team.jpg";

const Thunderbird = projects.find(
  (project) => project.slug === "ubc-thunderbird",
);

if (!Thunderbird) {
  throw new Error(`No project found with slug "ubc-thunderbird"`);
}
---

<div class="bg-white">
  <MainLayout title="UBC Thunderbird">
    <article class="container">
      <section>
        <a
          href="/#featured-work"
          class="icon-link icon-link-hover mb-3"
          style="--bs-icon-link-transform: translate3d(-.125rem, 0, 0);"
        >
          <i class="bi bi-chevron-left"></i> Back to work
        </a>
        <SkillList skills={Thunderbird.skills} />
        <h1>{Thunderbird.name}</h1>
        <p class="lead" style={{ textWrap: "balance" }}>
          Thunderbird was a robotic sailboat I revived solo for <a
            href="https://www.ubcsailbot.org/"
          >
            UBC Sailbot
          </a>, restoring an inherited codebase to defend the team's win at the
          2014 International Robotic Sailing Regatta.
        </p>
        <div class="text-secondary d-flex flex-wrap dot-list mb-3">
          <span
            ><i class="bi bi-calendar-range"></i>{" "}<DateRange
              start={Thunderbird.dates.start}
              end={Thunderbird.dates.end}
            /></span
          >
          <span
            ><i class="bi bi-braces"></i>{" "}Solo developer: revamped and
            validated the inherited codebase</span
          >
        </div>
        <figure class="mb-3 mx-auto d-block text-center">
          <Image
            src={image_thunderbird_pose}
            alt="Myself posing with Thunderbird during testing"
            inferSize
            class="img-fluid rounded-4 border mx-auto d-block"
            style="max-height: 60vh; width: auto;"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-secondary small mt-1 mb-0">
            Myself posing with Thunderbird during testing
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        {
          Thunderbird.link && (
            <div class="d-grid col d-lg-flex justify-content-center align-items-center mb-4">
              <a
                href={Thunderbird.link.url}
                class="btn btn-primary btn-lg px-3 me-md-2 d-flex gap-2"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                {Thunderbird.link.label}
                <i class="bi bi-chevron-right" />
              </a>
            </div>
          )
        }
      </section>

      <section class="mb-5">
        <h2>The Problem</h2>
        <p>
          In 2014, I was part of the UBC Sailbot student team, a group that
          built robotic sailboats. Whereas the majority of the team was in
          design mode for a future boat, I alone was tasked to revamp and
          restore the previous year's boat, Thunderbird, to compete in the
          annual <a href="https://www.sailbot.org/"
            >International Robotic Sailing Regatta</a
          >.
        </p>
        <p>The main issues with the previous boat were:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            The core contributors to the codebase had just graduated, <em
              class="fw-bold"
              >leaving code without meaningful context transfer</em
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            Because of this, code needed to be understood so sponsor
            presentations could come from <em class="fw-bold"
              >a place of genuine knowledge</em
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            In addition, the IRSR had updated challenges which required <em
              class="fw-bold">new functionality to be implemented</em
            >
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-5">
        <h2>Approach</h2>
        <p>
          Luckily, the boat I inherited was already a winning vessel (it won in
          2013). One of the core factors in their success that year was testing
          with a simulated boat. Therefore I set out to ensure the simulation
          code, as well as the controls code, were all understood. I did this by
          documenting code sections systematically on each system, which also
          created resources for knowledge transfer to the rest of the team.
        </p>
        <p>
          To validate my findings, I ran extensive simulation and field testing
          to capture behaviour and reverse-engineer code. Eventually I learned
          that everything was implemented in C on an Arduino for controls while
          a Raspberry Pi handled navigation in Python.
        </p>
        <figure class="mb-3">
          <Image
            src={image_thunderbird_sea}
            alt="Thunderbird sailing autonomously during field testing on open water"
            inferSize
            class="img-fluid rounded-3 border"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-secondary small mt-1 mb-0">
            Field testing Thunderbird's autonomous sailing
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>In summary:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            By being thorough in documenting code behaviour, I created knowledge
            resources that directly supported other members and myself for our
            presentations during demos
          </li>
          <li>
            Frequent testing allowed me to understand the control system and
            implement new behaviours correctly
          </li>
          <li>
            For instance, I expanded on the code by implementing the box
            challenge, where the boat had to hold its position within a
            specified box
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-5">
        <h2>Result</h2>
        <p>
          On competition day, while the team attended the competition, I watched
          the event nervously as the solo developer of the changes to the
          inherited codebase. Fortunately, it was exhilarating to see the boat
          pass all challenges, especially the new box challenge which other
          teams struggled with.
        </p>
        <div
          class="p-4 bg-primary-subtle border border-primary-subtle rounded-3 mb-3"
        >
          <ul class="list-unstyled d-flex flex-column gap-3 mb-0">
            <li class="d-flex align-items-center gap-2">
              <i class="bi bi-check2-circle fs-4 text-primary flex-shrink-0"
              ></i>
              <span>
                <strong>Passed every challenge</strong> — including the new box challenge
                that tripped up other teams
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="d-flex align-items-center gap-2">
              <i class="bi bi-trophy fs-4 text-primary flex-shrink-0"></i>
              <span>
                <strong>Perfect 50/50 finish</strong> — continuing UBC Sailbot's win
                streak at IRSR 2014
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="d-flex align-items-center gap-2">
              <i class="bi bi-journal-text fs-4 text-primary flex-shrink-0"></i>
              <span>
                <strong>Durable knowledge transfer</strong> — documentation and testing
                resources that outlived the competition
              </span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="mb-3">
          <Image
            src={image_ubc_sailbot_team}
            alt="The UBC Sailbot team celebrating their win at the 2014 International Robotic Sailing Regatta"
            inferSize
            class="img-fluid rounded-3 border"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-secondary small mt-1 mb-0">
            The UBC Sailbot team celebrating IRSR 2014
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p class="fst-italic">
          I am proud that my contributions led to another perfect 50/50 win at
          IRSR 2014, continuing a team streak of perfection. Not only am I
          grateful to be part of that winning legacy, I am honoured to have
          contributed my knowledge to the team in a durable, tangible way.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
    <div class="border border-top bg-secondary-subtle">
      <CTABanner />
    </div>
  </MainLayout>
</div>
